<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="chlorophyll a biosynthesis (from chlorophyllide a₂) I" organism="ARA" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5086" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: Chlorophylls Chlorophyll| is a green photosynthetic pigment found in photosynthetic plants, algae, and bacteria. Its name is derived from ancient Greek: &lt;i&gt;chloros&lt;/i&gt; = green and &lt;i&gt;phyllon&lt;/i&gt; = leaf. Chlorophyll functions as the main light harvesting pigment in photosynthesis, capturing photons and transfering them to the reaction centers. Chlorophyll absorbs mostly in the blue and, to a lesser extent, red portions of the electromagnetic spectrum, hence its intense green color. There are several variants of chlorophyll (|FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-A|, |FRAME: CPD-10334|, |FRAME: CHLOROPHYLL-B|, |FRAME: CPD-18895|, |FRAME: CPD-10336|, |FRAME: CPD-10337|, |FRAME: CPD-10335|, |FRAME: CPD-18896|, |FRAME: CPD-18897| and |FRAME: CPD-18898|). |FRAME:CHLOROPHYLL-A &quot;Chlorophyll &lt;i&gt;a&lt;/i&gt;&quot;| is found in all green plants, cyanobacteria, euglenozoa, and all photosynthetic algae. In these organisms, the pigment is found mostly in its monovinyl form (a vinyl group at C-3 and an ethyl group at C-8). However, its biosynthetic pathway involves largely divinyl intermediates (vinyl groups at both C-3 and C-8). Two different enzymes are known to catalyze the reduction of the C-8 vinyl group. This pathway describes |FRAME: EC-1.3.1.75|. The gene encoding this enzyme has been discovered in |FRAME: TAX-3702| and named |FRAME: G-9133| |CITS: [15632054]|. Homologues of the gene were initially found in the genomes of higher plants and green algae. A bacterial homolog was later found in phototrophic bacterium |FRAME: TAX-1097| and named |FRAME:G-10101| |CITS: [17148453]|. The last step in the biosynthesis of chlorophyll is the esterification of a side chain. The enzyme responsible for the esterification reaction (|FRAME: EC-2.5.1.62|) prefers phytyl diphosphate, but can also use geranylgeranyl diphosphate, in which case the geranylgeranyl side-chain is subsequently reduced to a phytyl chain by |FRAME: EC-1.3.1.83|.</Description>
